#!/usr/bin/env node

// Generates sector arc and leg line geometries from LIGHTS features in a GeoJSON file.
//
// Usage: bin/generate-sector-arcs <lights.geojson> <output.geojson>
//          [--minzoom N] [--maxzoom N] [--top-zoom N]
//
// Reads LIGHTS point features with SECTR1/SECTR2 attributes and produces
// LineString features for:
//   - Sector arcs (colored arcs between the two bearings)
//   - Sector leg lines (dashed lines from the light outward at each bearing)
//
// The output layer is named _LIGHTS_SECTORS and is consumed by the MapLibre
// style generated by the LIGHTS06 CSP.
//
// ONE COPY PER ZOOM, and why.
//
// A sector arc is a SCREEN-SIZE figure: S-52 draws it at a fixed 20 mm from the
// light whatever the scale, because it is a legend for the sector bearings and
// not a claim about where the light is visible. Geometry in a vector tile is
// geographic, so "20 mm" only holds at the one zoom the metres were computed
// for. This generator used to compute them once, at the cell's own band
// maxzoom, which put a band-3 cell's legs at 19.1 km and a band-5 cell's at
// 2.4 km -- the SAME figure, eight times the size, on two charts of the same
// water. Each was right at exactly one zoom and wrong by a factor of two per
// level either side of it, and SCAMIN then withheld the whole layer until the
// figure was already 2-4x oversize (Fiddle Reef).
//
// So each light emits one arc and two legs PER ZOOM, sized for that zoom, and
// each copy is confined to its own zoom's tiles by a per-feature `tippecanoe`
// member (`{minzoom: z, maxzoom: z}`) -- the same "one field per zoom, tagged
// minzoom=maxzoom=z, coarse fields swap out as finer ones arrive" pattern
// seascape uses for its sounding lattice.
//
// WHAT THIS COSTS PER TILE. Below the band maxzoom it is one copy per tile: a
// z9 tile of a band-5 cell carries the z9 copy and nothing else. The
// band-maxzoom tile is the exception and carries OVERZOOM_LEVELS + 1 of them
// (the maxzoom copy plus every overzoom copy, which have nowhere else to live
// -- see the first bound below), so the deepest real tile's share of this layer
// multiplies by four. It is affordable because these are three-to-a-hundred-
// point line strings and because a cell has tens of sectored lights, not
// thousands; it is NOT flat, and a claim that it was is what this paragraph
// replaces.
//
// Three bounds on the range.
//
// * OVERZOOM_LEVELS at the top. tippecanoe writes no tile above the cell's band
//   maxzoom, and both `tile-join --overzoom` and the browser fill those levels
//   by SCALING the deepest real tile -- which scales the figure with it, 2x per
//   level. The copies for maxzoom+1..maxzoom+3 are therefore stamped to live in
//   the band-maxzoom tile alongside the maxzoom copy, and ride the scaling up
//   into the levels they were sized for. They are told apart at draw time by
//   `_z`, which is why the style selects on it (see `sectorArcLayers`); without
//   that selection all four would draw at once.
// * --top-zoom, THE PUBLICATION CEILING, over the top of it. The tileset a
//   browser actually mounts is the national join, and that join is capped at
//   the MINIMUM band maxzoom across its inputs (z11 today: only a berthing-band
//   region reaches z12, and synthesizing z12 for the six regions that do not was
//   what OOM-killed the join). Every tile above the cap is deleted, and with it
//   every copy stamped into one. A band-6 cell's z12..z15 copies all live in its
//   z12 tile, so on the published archive they do not exist -- and because the
//   style matches `_z` EXACTLY against `min(floor(zoom), _zmax)`, a `_zmax` of
//   15 asked at z12 for a copy nothing could serve and the sectors of every
//   band-6 cell went blank from z12 up. So the caller passes the ceiling, no
//   copy is authored above it, and `_zmax` is the deepest copy that survives
//   publication rather than the deepest one the band could in principle carry.
//   The cost is paid at the top of the ladder, deliberately and uniformly: past
//   the ceiling EVERY band's figure rides the browser's scaling, because past
//   the ceiling every cell is being served from the same overzoomed z11 tile.
//   Keeping the band-5 overzoom copies while the band-6 ones died would have
//   drawn a correctly sized arc on one cell and an 8x one on the cell next to it
//   at the same zoom, which is worse than both being scaled together. Raising
//   the ceiling (when every band-6 region reaches z12) restores per-zoom sizing
//   up to the new one; it is a single named variable in bin/s57-to-tiles.
// * MAX_FIGURE_METRES at the bottom. Small zooms are the same arithmetic run
//   the other way: a 20 mm arc is ~5900 km at z0. SCAMIN would usually withhold
//   it, but `scaleFilter` passes any feature that carries no SCAMIN at all, so
//   "usually" is not a bound. A copy whose figure would exceed the cap is not
//   emitted, and no copy exists below the smallest zoom that fits -- which is
//   what the layer was already worth at those scales, the old copies being
//   sized for maxzoom and therefore sub-pixel.
//
//   WORTH NAMING, because it is a GAP and not a rounding: that floor is a
//   latitude away from moving and does not move with the band at all. A 25 mm
//   leg first fits under the cap at z7 at 48N and not until z8 on the equator,
//   so a band-1 cell (maxzoom 4) contributes exactly one copy at 48N -- z7,
//   riding its z4 tile into the overzoomed levels -- and none whatever on the
//   equator, and a band-2 cell (maxzoom 6) contributes two or three. Neither
//   band gets a copy at any zoom its own chart is native to, so on those cells
//   the sector legend does not appear until the zoom where a 25 mm figure is
//   under 50 km. That is the right reading (an overview chart has no room for a
//   sector legend beside every light) but there is no z0..z6 copy to fall back
//   to, and a tile dump of a band-1 cell showing only `_z: 7` is correct.

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from "fs";

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
let inputPath, outputPath;
let minzoom = 0,
  maxzoom = 16;
// The publication ceiling, when the caller knows one. Null means "no ceiling
// above the band's own overzoom range", which is the right default for a
// standalone run over one cell: it reproduces the behaviour of every caller
// that predates the flag.
let topZoomOption = null;

// Parse args
const positional = [];
for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === "--minzoom" && args[i + 1]) {
    minzoom = parseInt(args[++i]);
  } else if (args[i] === "--maxzoom" && args[i + 1]) {
    maxzoom = parseInt(args[++i]);
  } else if (args[i] === "--top-zoom" && args[i + 1]) {
    topZoomOption = parseInt(args[++i]);
  } else {
    positional.push(args[i]);
  }
}
inputPath = positional[0];
outputPath = positional[1];

if (!inputPath || !outputPath) {
  console.error(
    "Usage: generate-sector-arcs <lights.geojson> <output.geojson> " +
      "[--minzoom N] [--maxzoom N] [--top-zoom N]",
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

// Screen mm to geographic degrees conversion.
// At a given zoom level, 1 pixel ≈ C * cos(lat) / (2^zoom * tileSize).
// We convert mm to pixels (1mm ≈ 96/25.4 px at standard DPI), then to degrees.
const TILE_SIZE = 512;
const EARTH_CIRCUMFERENCE = 2 * Math.PI * 6378137; // meters

// Zoom levels past the cell's band maxzoom that get their own copy. Clients
// overzoom 3-5 levels past a band-5 cell's z11 (see bin/s57-to-tiles step 4);
// three is where the copies stop earning their weight, and past it the style's
// clamp holds the last one rather than dropping the layer.
const OVERZOOM_LEVELS = 3;

// Largest figure worth emitting, in metres. A sector legend bigger than this is
// no longer a legend, it is a shape drawn across the chart: the widest nominal
// range S-57 carries is on the order of 25-28 nm (~52 km), so nothing real is
// lost, and the cap is what keeps the low-zoom copies (5900 km at z0) from
// being written at all.
const MAX_FIGURE_METRES = 50000;

// S-52's screen-size convention for the radial legs. The arc's own radius is
// 20 mm, or 25 mm where _EXTENDED_ARC separates two co-located sectors, so the
// legs always reach past the arc and close the figure.
const LEG_LENGTH_MM = 25;

function mmToMeters(mm, zoom, lat) {
  // pixels per mm at standard screen DPI (96)
  const pxPerMm = 96 / 25.4;
  const px = mm * pxPerMm;
  // meters per pixel at this zoom and latitude
  const metersPerPx =
    (EARTH_CIRCUMFERENCE * Math.cos((lat * Math.PI) / 180)) /
    (Math.pow(2, zoom) * TILE_SIZE);
  return px * metersPerPx;
}

function metersToDegreesLon(meters, lat) {
  return (
    meters / ((EARTH_CIRCUMFERENCE / 360) * Math.cos((lat * Math.PI) / 180))
  );
}

function metersToDegreesLat(meters) {
  return meters / (EARTH_CIRCUMFERENCE / 360);
}

// Flip a bearing from seaward to from-light (add 180, normalize to [0, 360))
function flipBearing(bearing) {
  return (((bearing + 180) % 360) + 360) % 360;
}

// Generate arc LineString as an array of [lon, lat] points
function generateArc(center, radiusMeters, startBearing, endBearing, steps) {
  const [lon, lat] = center;
  // Ensure endBearing > startBearing for clockwise sweep
  if (endBearing <= startBearing) endBearing += 360;

  const points = [];
  for (let i = 0; i <= steps; i++) {
    const bearing = startBearing + (endBearing - startBearing) * (i / steps);
    const bearingRad = (bearing * Math.PI) / 180;
    // Bearing: 0=north, 90=east
    const dLat = radiusMeters * Math.cos(bearingRad);
    const dLon = radiusMeters * Math.sin(bearingRad);
    points.push([
      lon + metersToDegreesLon(dLon, lat),
      lat + metersToDegreesLat(dLat),
    ]);
  }
  return points;
}

// Generate a leg line from center outward at a bearing
function generateLeg(center, lengthMeters, bearing) {
  const [lon, lat] = center;
  const bearingRad = (bearing * Math.PI) / 180;
  const dLat = lengthMeters * Math.cos(bearingRad);
  const dLon = lengthMeters * Math.sin(bearingRad);
  return [
    [lon, lat],
    [lon + metersToDegreesLon(dLon, lat), lat + metersToDegreesLat(dLat)],
  ];
}

// Determine the S-52 colour token from COLOUR attribute
// COLOUR is a comma-separated string of S-57 colour codes
function sectorColourToken(colour) {
  if (!colour) return "CHMGD";
  const codes = String(colour).split(",").map(Number);
  const has = (v) => codes.includes(v);

  if (has(1) && has(3)) return "LITRD"; // white+red
  if (has(3)) return "LITRD"; // red
  if (has(1) && has(4)) return "LITGN"; // white+green
  if (has(4)) return "LITGN"; // green
  if (has(11)) return "LITYW"; // orange
  if (has(6)) return "LITYW"; // yellow
  if (has(1)) return "LITYW"; // white
  return "CHMGD"; // default magenta
}

// Determine if arc should use dashed style (LITVIS 3=faint, 7=obscured, 8=partially obscured)
function isFaintOrObscured(litvis) {
  if (!litvis) return false;
  const codes = String(litvis).split(",").map(Number);
  return codes.some((v) => v === 3 || v === 7 || v === 8);
}

// The deepest zoom that gets a copy, and therefore the `_zmax` every copy
// carries. Past the band maxzoom the copies share the band-maxzoom tile; see
// the header.
//
// Bounded by the publication ceiling when the caller passes one -- but only
// for bands whose own maxzoom exceeds it. A copy for level z is stamped into
// the tile at min(z, maxzoom), so it survives the national join exactly when
// that tile does: a band at or under the ceiling keeps every overzoom copy
// (they all ride the band-maxzoom tile, which survives), while a band above
// it loses everything stamped past the ceiling, and `_zmax` must not PROMISE
// those copies: the style matches `_z` exactly against
// `min(floor(zoom), _zmax)`, so a `_zmax` naming a copy that did not survive
// is a zoom at which nothing draws.
const ceiling = topZoomOption ?? Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
const topZoom = maxzoom <= ceiling ? maxzoom + OVERZOOM_LEVELS : ceiling;

// The zooms whose copy of an `mm`-sized figure is small enough to be worth
// writing, shallowest first. The size shrinks by half per level, so the cap can
// only ever remove a prefix.
function emittedZooms(mm, lat) {
  const zooms = [];
  for (let z = minzoom; z <= topZoom; z++) {
    if (mmToMeters(mm, z, lat) <= MAX_FIGURE_METRES) zooms.push(z);
  }
  return zooms;
}

// The `tippecanoe` member for a copy sized for zoom `z`.
//
// NOT a property: tippecanoe reads this member off the Feature itself, and it
// is documented to confine the feature to "the vector tiles for zoom levels
// <minzoom> through <maxzoom>". Equal bounds put each copy in exactly one
// zoom's tiles, which is what keeps a per-zoom layer the same weight per tile
// as the single-copy layer it replaces.
//
// Above the band maxzoom there is no tile to be confined to, so those copies
// are stamped for the deepest tile there is and travel into the overzoomed
// levels inside it.
function tippecanoeFor(z) {
  const tiled = Math.min(z, maxzoom);
  return { minzoom: tiled, maxzoom: tiled };
}

// Main
const input = JSON.parse(readFileSync(inputPath, "utf8"));
const features = [];

for (const feature of input.features) {
  if (!feature.geometry || feature.geometry.type !== "Point") continue;

  const props = feature.properties || {};
  const sectr1 = props.SECTR1;
  const sectr2 = props.SECTR2;

  // Directional lights (CATLIT 1 or 16) with ORIENT: generate a bearing line
  const catlit = String(props.CATLIT || "")
    .split(",")
    .map(Number);
  const isDirectional =
    (catlit.includes(1) || catlit.includes(16)) &&
    props.ORIENT != null &&
    !catlit.includes(8) &&
    !catlit.includes(9) &&
    !catlit.includes(11);

  if (isDirectional) {
    const center = feature.geometry.coordinates;
    const lat = center[1];
    const bearing = flipBearing(props.ORIENT);

    const common = {
      ...(props.LNAM != null && { LNAM: props.LNAM }),
      ...(props.SCAMIN != null && { SCAMIN: props.SCAMIN }),
      ...(props.INTU != null && { INTU: props.INTU }),
    };

    if (props.VALNMR != null) {
      // A nominal range is a REAL distance -- how far the light is seen -- so
      // this leg is geographic and means the same thing at every scale. One
      // copy, and no `_z`: the style draws a figure without one at every zoom.
      features.push({
        type: "Feature",
        geometry: {
          type: "LineString",
          coordinates: generateLeg(center, props.VALNMR * 1852, bearing),
        },
        properties: {
          ...common,
          _type: "leg",
        },
      });
    } else {
      // No range to draw, so the leg falls back to the 25 mm screen-size
      // convention and is per-zoom like the sector figures.
      const zooms = emittedZooms(LEG_LENGTH_MM, lat);
      const zmax = zooms[zooms.length - 1];
      for (const z of zooms) {
        features.push({
          type: "Feature",
          tippecanoe: tippecanoeFor(z),
          geometry: {
            type: "LineString",
            coordinates: generateLeg(
              center,
              mmToMeters(LEG_LENGTH_MM, z, lat),
              bearing,
            ),
          },
          properties: {
            ...common,
            _type: "leg",
            _z: z,
            _zmax: zmax,
          },
        });
      }
    }
  }

  if (sectr1 == null || sectr2 == null) continue;

  // "No sector" test: both 0, or difference is 0 or 360
  const diff = Math.abs(sectr2 - sectr1);
  if (diff === 0 || diff === 360) continue;

  const center = feature.geometry.coordinates;
  const lat = center[1];

  // Flip bearings from seaward to from-light
  const leg1Bearing = flipBearing(sectr1);
  const leg2Bearing = flipBearing(sectr2);

  // Arc radius: 20mm default (extended to 25mm handled later via co-location detection)
  const arcRadiusMm = props._EXTENDED_ARC ? 25 : 20;

  // Arc sweep: number of steps proportional to arc size
  let arcStart = leg1Bearing;
  let arcEnd = leg2Bearing;
  if (arcEnd <= arcStart) arcEnd += 360;
  const arcDegrees = arcEnd - arcStart;
  const steps = Math.max(8, Math.ceil(arcDegrees / 3));

  const colourToken = sectorColourToken(props.COLOUR);
  const obscured = isFaintOrObscured(props.LITVIS);

  // Common properties carried through to tiles
  const common = {
    ...(props.LNAM != null && { LNAM: props.LNAM }),
    ...(props.SCAMIN != null && { SCAMIN: props.SCAMIN }),
    ...(props.INTU != null && { INTU: props.INTU }),
  };

  // The zoom range is taken from the LARGEST of the three figures so the arc
  // and its two legs always appear and disappear together: a zoom that carried
  // the legs but not the arc would draw an unbounded pair of rays.
  const zooms = emittedZooms(Math.max(arcRadiusMm, LEG_LENGTH_MM), lat);
  const zmax = zooms[zooms.length - 1];

  for (const z of zooms) {
    const stamp = tippecanoeFor(z);
    const perZoom = { _z: z, _zmax: zmax };

    // Arc feature (OUTLW underlay is handled by having two style layers)
    features.push({
      type: "Feature",
      tippecanoe: stamp,
      geometry: {
        type: "LineString",
        coordinates: generateArc(
          center,
          mmToMeters(arcRadiusMm, z, lat),
          arcStart,
          arcEnd,
          steps,
        ),
      },
      properties: {
        ...common,
        ...perZoom,
        _type: "arc",
        _colour: colourToken,
        _style: obscured ? "DASH" : "SOLD",
      },
    });

    const legLengthMeters = mmToMeters(LEG_LENGTH_MM, z, lat);
    for (const bearing of [leg1Bearing, leg2Bearing]) {
      features.push({
        type: "Feature",
        tippecanoe: stamp,
        geometry: {
          type: "LineString",
          coordinates: generateLeg(center, legLengthMeters, bearing),
        },
        properties: {
          ...common,
          ...perZoom,
          _type: "leg",
        },
      });
    }
  }
}

const output = {
  type: "FeatureCollection",
  features,
};

writeFileSync(outputPath, JSON.stringify(output));
const arcs = features.filter((f) => f.properties._type === "arc").length;
const legs = features.length - arcs;
// The counts are per-zoom copies, not distinct figures, so say which zooms they
// span: a run that suddenly emits one zoom's worth is the bug this replaced.
console.error(
  `Generated ${features.length} sector features (${arcs} arcs, ${legs} legs) ` +
    `across z${minzoom}-${topZoom}`,
);
